---------------------------------------------------------

Trivial bit of code that will print out the current
gmp and mpfr library versions. 

Also for fun we print out pi and e with whatever
precision we can get up to a billion digits or so.

Even the slowest machine that we can find will 
easily compute pi and e to a million digits in less
than an hour : 

$ /usr/bin/time -p /usr/bin/nice -n +15 ./mpfr_ver 3322000 2>&1 | tail -4 

real 2393.49
user 2363.55
sys 3.75

--- count the distribution of digits using awk and sed ---

$ ./mpfr_ver 3321929 > pi1000000.dat 2>&1 ; grep '^pi' pi1000000.dat | head -1 | cut -c11- | cut -c1-1000001 > pi1000000.ocd; cat count_dig.sh | sed 's/TEMPLATE/pi1000000/' > $TMPDIR/do_count.sh ; chmod 755 $TMPDIR/do_count.sh ; $TMPDIR/do_count.sh | awk 'BEGIN{ ttl=0;print "\n\ndigit    count\n--------------" }{ printf("%5i    %6i\n", $1 , $2 ); ttl=ttl + $2 }END{printf ("--------------\n         %7i\n\n", ttl) }'; grep '^time' pi1000000.dat


digit    count
--------------
    0     99959
    1     99757
    2    100026
    3    100230
    4    100230
    5    100359
    6     99548
    7     99800
    8     99985
    9    100106
--------------
         1000000

time for mpfr_cont_pi() was 1800590010 nsecs

--------- on a Gentoo SPARC64 server ------------

$ CC='/usr/bin/nice -n +19 /usr/bin/gcc'
$ export CC

$ CFLAGS='-std=iso9899:1999 -m64 -g -O0 -pedantic -pedantic-errors -fno-builtin -fno-fast-math -mhard-float -mno-app-regs -mflat -msoft-quad-float -mno-unaligned-doubles -mno-faster-structs -mcpu=ultrasparc -mtune=ultrasparc -mno-vis -mno-cbcond -mno-fmaf -mno-fsmuld -mno-popc -mno-subxc -mcmodel=medany -mmemory-model=tso'
$ export CFLAGS 

$ CPPFLAGS='-D_LARGEFILE64_SOURCE -D_XOPEN_SOURCE=600'
$ export CPPFLAGS 
 
$ $CC $CFLAGS $CPPFLAGS -I/usr/include -c -o mpfr_pi_e.o mpfr_pi_e.c
$ $CC $CFLAGS $CPPFLAGS -c -o timediff.o ../time_and_date/timediff.c 

$ $CC $CFLAGS $CPPFLAGS -L/usr/lib64 -o mpfr_pi_e mpfr_pi_e.o timediff.o -lm -lgmp -lmpfr 
 
